WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.960 --> 00:00:05.920
To Kazakhstan now, which is still reeling&nbsp;
after a week of deadly unrest that left more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.920 --> 00:00:11.360
than 200 people dead. The unrest exposed a&nbsp;
leadership struggle. To tighten his grip,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:11.360 --> 00:00:14.800
President Kassym-Jomart Tokayev&nbsp;
has now taken over the leadership&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:14.800 --> 00:00:19.040
of the ruling party - sidelining his&nbsp;
mentor and the former head of state.&nbsp;

00:00:19.920 --> 00:00:23.760
Protests started over a rise in fuel&nbsp;
prices at the beginning of the year,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:23.760 --> 00:00:30.320
but then spiraled into mass disturbances -&nbsp;
notably in Kazakhstan's biggest city, Almaty.&nbsp;

00:00:30.320 --> 00:00:34.720
Security forces are accused of using&nbsp;
excessive force to put down the unrest.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:34.720 --> 00:00:39.360
Many in Almaty still can't believe what&nbsp;
happened. DW's Emily Sherwin has more.&nbsp;

00:00:42.880 --> 00:00:48.880
A city turned into a warzone. This is&nbsp;
Kazakhstan's commercial center, Almaty,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:48.880 --> 00:00:55.360
in early January. What started as anger&nbsp;
over rising fuel prices soon escalated&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:55.360 --> 00:01:00.880
into full blown riots, arson and looting.&nbsp;
Many locals tell us they are still in shock.&nbsp;

00:01:02.640 --> 00:01:04.880
"What were they even trying&nbsp;
to take here? Ice cream?"&nbsp;

00:01:05.760 --> 00:01:12.400
Botagoz Seidakhmetova's apartment is right on the&nbsp;
corner of Almaty's Republic Square, the location&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:12.400 --> 00:01:18.160
of the city administration building – and the&nbsp;
epicenter of the recent unrest. Out of her window,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:18.160 --> 00:01:24.960
she witnessed peaceful protests turning violent.
"I guess I went a bit nuts. I opened my window&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:24.960 --> 00:01:28.240
and started shouting at the rioters:&nbsp;
You should be ashamed of yourselves,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:28.240 --> 00:01:31.920
what are you doing? The people who opened&nbsp;
these shops are people just like you!"&nbsp;

00:01:33.440 --> 00:01:35.040
Rebuilding will take time.&nbsp;

00:01:37.120 --> 00:01:41.760
The authorities estimate the value of the&nbsp;
damage to local businesses in the country&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:41.760 --> 00:01:48.320
at over 150 million euros. Nearly 2,000&nbsp;
shops suffered at the hands of the rioters.&nbsp;

00:01:50.880 --> 00:01:55.360
Artist and designer Mimi Ilnitskaya&nbsp;
has been picking up the pieces.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:55.360 --> 00:01:59.360
She is convinced that her workshop&nbsp;
and office in Almaty's city center&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:59.360 --> 00:02:04.880
was set on fire. During the protests,&nbsp;
Ilnitskaya was at home with her children.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:04.880 --> 00:02:08.880
The destruction she found when she&nbsp;
returned to her workplace shocked her.&nbsp;

00:02:11.600 --> 00:02:15.520
"When I was coming up here, I was worried&nbsp;
that I hadn't taken the key to my office.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:15.520 --> 00:02:19.200
But then I came in and realized I don't&nbsp;
need a key, there's not even a desk.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:19.200 --> 00:02:21.840
I never thought my own&nbsp;
people could do this to me."&nbsp;

00:02:23.280 --> 00:02:27.360
Authorities say over two hundred&nbsp;
people died during the riots.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:27.360 --> 00:02:32.800
The government insists foreign terrorists&nbsp;
and extremists are to blame for the unrest.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:32.800 --> 00:02:38.720
But some experts believe the ruling elite used the&nbsp;
protests as part of an internal power struggle.&nbsp;

00:02:40.080 --> 00:02:44.640
Almaty resident Botagoz Seidakhmetova&nbsp;
tells us she can't forget what happened.&nbsp;

00:02:45.680 --> 00:02:50.160
"I'm still scared, even now. It's a deep,&nbsp;
animal fear - because I suddenly felt that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:50.160 --> 00:02:54.240
we are living in anarchy - rather than&nbsp;
in a state. We don't have a government&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:54.240 --> 00:03:00.560
that can protect me and other normal people."
The Kazakh authorities say that the situation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:00.560 --> 00:03:15.440
across the country is back under control. But&nbsp;
trust in the government may also need rebuilding.

